---
import Base from '../../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import UkSalaryCalculator from '../../../../components/UkSalaryCalculator';
import { ukSalaryRates as rates } from '../../../../calc/uk/salary';
import '../../../../styles/calculator.css';
import '../../../../styles/uk-salary.css';
const gbp = (value: number) => new Intl.NumberFormat('en-GB', { style: 'currency', currency: 'GBP', maximumFractionDigits: 0 }).format(value);
const sources = [
  { title: 'HMRC 2026/27 employer rates and thresholds', url: rates.source_url },
  { title: 'Personal Allowance and its income taper', url: rates.personal_allowance.source_url },
  { title: 'Personal Allowance rounding: Income Tax Act 2007, section 35', url: rates.personal_allowance.rounding_source_url },
  { title: 'Scottish Income Tax: current rates', url: rates.income_tax.scotland.crosscheck_url },
  { title: 'Employee National Insurance rates and official example', url: rates.employee_ni.source_url },
  { title: 'HMRC earnings-period and calculation guidance', url: rates.employee_ni.period_source_url },
  { title: 'HMRC worked Income Tax examples: Tables 2a and 2b', url: rates.official_examples.income_tax_source_url },
];
---
<Base title="UK Salary Calculator 2026/27 — Monthly Pay Beta | CalcsDesk" description="Model 2026/27 Income Tax and category A employee NI for 12 equal monthly salaries in England, Wales, Northern Ireland or Scotland. Limited beta, not PAYE payroll." breadcrumb="Salary calculator" ancestors={[{ label: 'United Kingdom', href: '/uk/' }]} developmentMessage="UK salary beta · limited monthly-pay model; human review pending" application={{ name: '2026/27 UK Monthly Salary Calculator', description: 'Limited model of annual salary Income Tax and monthly category A employee National Insurance for 12 equal monthly payments.' }}>
  <header class="calculator-intro"><p class="eyebrow">United Kingdom / Pay / 2026–27</p><h1>Monthly salary.<br /> <span class="accent">Tax and NI, separated.</span></h1><p class="lead">Model Income Tax and employee National Insurance for 12 equal monthly gross payments. Choose England, Wales, Northern Ireland or Scotland. The Income Tax figure is an annual model spread across 12 months; actual PAYE deductions can differ.</p><div class="verification-line"><span class="status-tag">Limited beta</span><span>Primary sources checked <time datetime="2026-11-02">2 November 2026</time> · human review pending</span><a href="#uk-sources">View sources ↓</a></div></header>
  <UkSalaryCalculator client:load />
  <noscript><div class="notice"><strong>JavaScript is needed to change the estimate.</strong><p>The assumptions, source tables and official-example checks below remain readable.</p></div></noscript>
  <section class="calculator-explainer" aria-labelledby="uk-method-title"><p class="eyebrow">Follow the model</p><h2 id="uk-method-title">Two calculations, two time bases.</h2><div class="explanation-grid"><article><span class="micro">01 / Annual Income Tax</span><h3>Annualise twelve equal pays.</h3><p>Multiply monthly gross pay by 12, apply the available Personal Allowance, then tax each remaining slice at the selected nation’s rate. Salary is assumed to be your entire adjusted net income. The standard allowance is {gbp(rates.personal_allowance.amount)}; it reduces above {gbp(rates.personal_allowance.taper_start)}. This is an annual salary-only model, with its monthly average rounded separately.</p></article><article><span class="micro">02 / Monthly employee NI</span><h3>Use the monthly earnings period.</h3><p>Category A NI is calculated on the monthly pay, using monthly thresholds. The model rounds that charge for each month and multiplies by 12 for the annual projection. It does not apply annual NI thresholds to a monthly-paid employee. Changing pay periods, bonuses or uneven pay can change the outcome.</p></article><article><span class="micro">03 / The displayed remainder</span><h3>Subtract the amounts shown.</h3><p>The remainder subtracts the rounded monthly Income Tax average and monthly employee NI from monthly gross pay. No pension, loan repayment, salary sacrifice, benefit, attachment of earnings or other deduction is included. Payroll uses tax codes, year-to-date history and rounding rules this model does not reproduce.</p></article></div></section>
  <section class="calculator-examples" aria-labelledby="uk-examples-title"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Official example checks</p><h2 id="uk-examples-title">HMRC’s salary-only examples.</h2></div><span class="section-caption">Component checks, not payslip approval</span></div><div class="example-grid"><article><span class="micro">HMRC Table 2a / £60,000 annual earnings</span><h3>£11,432 Income Tax.</h3><p>For England, Wales and Northern Ireland, HMRC’s example has £47,430 after the Personal Allowance. It splits this into £37,700 at the basic rate and £9,730 at the higher rate. The resulting tax is £7,540 plus £3,892.</p></article><article><span class="micro">HMRC Table 2b / Scotland / £60,000</span><h3>£13,182 at HMRC’s displayed precision.</h3><p>The Scottish example splits £47,430 into £3,967, £12,989, £14,136 and £16,338 across four bands. HMRC displays whole-pound values. This model produces £13,182.05, which rounds to the published £13,182 total.</p></article></div><p class="source-footnote">Automated tests reproduce both sourced component examples and GOV.UK’s category A weekly NI example (£1,000 pay → £58.66 NI). They do not verify a complete PAYE payslip. Human review and the full release checklist remain pending; this calculator stays noindex. <a class="text-link" href={rates.official_examples.income_tax_source_url}>Read HMRC Tables 2a and 2b ↗</a></p></section>
  <section class="rate-section" id="uk-sources" aria-labelledby="uk-rates-title"><p class="eyebrow">Check the source data</p><h2 id="uk-rates-title">2026/27 salary rates used.</h2><p class="source-footnote">Effective {rates.effective_from} to {rates.effective_to}. Primary-source check {rates.verified_on}; human review pending. Band limits below are taxable salary after the available Personal Allowance.</p>{Object.values(rates.income_tax).map(schedule => <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label={`${schedule.label} annual Income Tax bands`}><table><caption>{schedule.label} · annual taxable salary</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Above</th><th scope="col">Up to</th><th scope="col">Rate</th></tr></thead><tbody>{schedule.brackets.map((band, index) => <tr><td>{gbp(index === 0 ? 0 : schedule.brackets[index - 1].up_to!)}</td><td>{band.up_to === null ? 'No upper limit' : gbp(band.up_to)}</td><td>{band.rate_bps / 100}%</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>)}<p class="source-footnote">Monthly category A employee NI: {rates.employee_ni.main_rate_bps / 100}% above {gbp(rates.employee_ni.monthly.primary_threshold)} up to {gbp(rates.employee_ni.monthly.upper_earnings_limit)}, then {rates.employee_ni.upper_rate_bps / 100}% above that. Employer NI is excluded.</p><div class="official-source-list"><h3>Primary sources</h3><ul>{sources.map(source => <li><a class="text-link" href={source.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{source.title} ↗<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a></li>)}</ul></div></section>
  <section class="calculator-faq" aria-labelledby="uk-faq-title"><h2 id="uk-faq-title">Before using the estimate.</h2><details><summary>Can I use this to check a payslip?</summary><p>Use it only for a limited planning comparison. Income Tax is not calculated using your tax code, prior earnings, prior deductions or cumulative PAYE history. NI uses a monthly category A earnings period, but the result excludes the other deductions on many payslips. For an actual payroll check, use <a class="text-link" href="https://www.gov.uk/government/collections/how-to-manually-check-your-payroll-calculations">HMRC’s payroll calculation resources</a>.</p></details><details><summary>What if my pension changes my taxable pay?</summary><p>This beta cannot model that situation. It assumes the same gross amount is taxable for Income Tax and NI. It excludes pension contributions and salary sacrifice, as well as student and postgraduate loans, bonuses, benefits, reliefs and other income.</p></details><details><summary>Does my workplace determine Scottish Income Tax?</summary><p>The selection is for the Income Tax rules that apply to you, not simply the location of an employer. Check <a class="text-link" href="https://www.gov.uk/scottish-income-tax">GOV.UK’s Scottish Income Tax guidance</a> if unsure. This tool does not determine tax residence.</p></details><details><summary>Are my salary figures stored or shared?</summary><p>Calculations run in this browser. The tool has no financial-input submission endpoint, automatic wage storage or share-link feature. Printing includes the visible estimate and assumptions. Refreshing resets these inputs.</p></details><details><summary>Why is this still a beta?</summary><p>Official rates and component examples have been checked and automated tests cover the limited model. Human verification, broader payroll cases and the full publication checklist remain outstanding. This page is noindex and makes no claim of professional review.</p></details></section>
  <nav class="calculator-related" aria-label="Related pages"><a href="/uk/">United Kingdom collection ↗</a><a href="/methodology/">How we check calculations ↗</a><a href="/team/">Review status ↗</a></nav>
</Base>
